Standalone CUI bar
==================
This is a separate X11/CUI program, inspired by the Waybar configuration in
../waybar. The old dwm bar and its tray are removed. The bar uses a small X11
property/message protocol with this dwm build to display and switch workspaces
and reserve space on each monitor (up to 16). The tray appears on the first
monitor because an XEmbed icon can only be hosted by one tray at a time. The
network, Bluetooth, audio, display mode and calendar control center is built
into the bar process. Its dashboard and calendar use rounded X Shape corners.
The 28px panel floats with rounded X Shape corners, an 8px
top gap and 10px side gaps; dwm reserves the full top gap plus panel height.

Build from the repository root (CMake, Git, a C compiler and X11 development
headers/libraries required):

    cmake -S config/cui-bar -B config/cui-bar/build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$HOME/.local"
    cmake --build config/cui-bar/build
    cmake --install config/cui-bar/build

Then rebuild/install dwm and restart it. When upgrading from an older version,
stop the old bar first (`pkill -x cui-bar`) so it doesn't keep the old IPC
protocol or retain the tray. If you want the separate CUI dashboard too,
build dwm with CUI=1 as described in ../dwm/README; otherwise use the normal
make build. Ensure ~/.local/bin is in the PATH of the dwm session, not just
an interactive terminal. dwm automatically launches `cui-bar` when installed,
unless an X11 tray already exists or DWM_NO_CUI_BAR=1 is set. You can instead
run `cui-bar` manually. It refuses to steal an existing tray's selection.
Existing autostart files are not changed. Stop an old tray/bar before testing.

The bar shows temperature, CPU, memory used in GiB, battery, workspace numbers,
volume, network state and connected Wi-Fi name, Bluetooth state, clock, XEmbed
tray icons, language, and lock. Hover over the stats or status modules for
more detail in square, bar-styled panels; CPU shows per-logical-CPU usage sampled from /proc/stat (not a
list of process threads), with the first 32 or fewer shown if the screen is
narrow. Memory is total minus MemAvailable, shown in binary GiB. Long Wi-Fi names are shortened to fit.
Stats and status come from /proc, /sys, nmcli, bluetoothctl, pactl and XKB.
Service commands run in a separate worker about every two seconds (plus command
time), and after actions, without blocking dwm or the bar's X event loop.
CPU, memory, temperature and clock refresh every second. CPU temperature uses
CPU-labelled hwmon sensors, then CPU thermal zones; set CUI_BAR_TEMP_PATH to a
specific readable sysfs temperature input if detection is unsuitable. A single-threaded software CUI
renderer draws only after changes or refreshes; no continuously running GPU
render loop is required. The open control popup refreshes four times per
second for interaction and animations. Narrow screens hide the left stats
when space is needed. Multi-monitor support requires dwm built with Xinerama.

Click a workspace to view it on that monitor. Click volume, network or
Bluetooth to open the bar's integrated CUI control popup on that monitor. Click
the clock to open its calendar, with previous, next and today controls.
The popup has a Home overview with Wi-Fi and Bluetooth switches, a volume
slider, and a display shortcut. Its pinned navigation keeps each section within
reach while scrolling. Wi-Fi networks and Bluetooth devices have separate
cards; choose Details on a card to manage it in place. It can scan, connect and disconnect
them,
control radios, adjust/mute audio, and select display resolutions and refresh
rates; Escape or Close dismisses it. Volume changes made while
another action runs are retained and sent when the worker becomes free. Right-click
volume to mute, scroll it to adjust by 2%, or right-click network/Bluetooth to
toggle the radio directly. Lock launches mel. NetworkManager/nmcli, BlueZ/
bluetoothctl and PulseAudio or PipeWire/pactl are needed for their respective
controls. Passwords are sent via a private pipe rather than command arguments;
WPA enterprise and hidden networks need Network settings. Bluetooth pairing
that requires confirmation or PIN entry needs a separate pairing agent.
Optional advanced settings buttons launch nm-connection-editor,
blueman-manager, and pavucontrol. Separate dwm dashboard shortcuts still work.
Set CUI_BAR_FONT to a font file to override the built-in mono bar font.

The tray implements legacy XEmbed icons, up to eight visible at once, fewer on narrow screens; it does
not implement D-Bus StatusNotifierItem/AppIndicator icons. Some applications
may therefore need an XEmbed mode or an additional SNI-to-XEmbed bridge. Tray
icons remain separate application windows and their appearance is controlled
by those applications. The software renderer currently requires a common
little-endian 32-bit X11 visual; unsupported visuals report an error and exit.

Monitor troubleshooting
-----------------------
Each panel is a separate X11 window, sized and placed from dwm's monitor IPC;
workspace clicks target that monitor. The bar does not assume that the X11
root window is a monitor. If no compatible IPC is available it waits instead
of drawing a desktop-wide fallback. After upgrading, install BOTH the new bar
and dwm and restart dwm; stop any older cui-bar process first. dwm must be
compiled with Xinerama support (the default in config.mk), and Xinerama must
report the displays as distinct screens. Mirrored displays or a desktop that
Xinerama reports as one screen will be treated as one monitor by dwm.

Display and wallpaper controls
------------------------------
Display modes use xrandr and save successful changes in the same
refreshrate.conf used by dwm's display switcher. Wallpaper selection remains
in the native dwm picker (Alt+Shift+W); it needs feh, Imlib2 and the Imlib2
loaders for the image formats used. It reads DWM_WALLPAPERS_DIR or
~/Downloads/wallpaper (with common Pictures/wallpapers fallbacks if empty).
The bar does not implement wallpaper controls.

Bluetooth authorization
-----------------------
The bar does not run as root or change system policy. BlueZ must be running,
the user must be in an active login session, and the session may need a polkit
authentication agent. Pairing that requires a PIN or confirmation also needs
a Bluetooth pairing agent such as blueman-applet. Start the appropriate agent
in your existing session startup if it is not already running; existing
startup files are never modified. Authorization errors are reported in the
popup; check `bluetoothctl show` and `loginctl session-status` when diagnosing.
